---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/aspe/aspe1400.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — the General Presentation Umbrella" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Fair Presentation" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Consistency of Presentation" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Aggregation and Materiality" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Offsetting Restrictions" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Comparative Information" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Identification of the Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "The Immateriality Override — a Practical Compliance Relief" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Going Concern — Cross-Referenced From Section 1000" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Worked Example — Applying the Immateriality Override" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Worked Example — Identifying the Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Relationship to IAS 1" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s17", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s18", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The overarching presentation principles beneath every ASPE statement</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 1400 SETS OUT the GENERAL standards GOVERNING how a COMPLETE set of FINANCIAL statements is PRESENTED — FAIR presentation, CONSISTENCY, aggregation, OFFSETTING, comparative INFORMATION, and CLEAR identification of the STATEMENTS themselves. THIS Section WORKS alongside the MORE detailed, statement-SPECIFIC Sections (1510, 1520, 1521, 1540) to FORM the COMPLETE presentation FRAMEWORK.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — the General Presentation Umbrella</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Applying to every complete set of ASPE financial statements</div><div class="principle">This Section applies to the PRESENTATION of a COMPLETE set of GENERAL-purpose FINANCIAL statements PREPARED in ACCORDANCE with ASPE — SETTING the GENERAL, OVERARCHING rules that APPLY REGARDLESS of the SPECIFIC statement BEING prepared.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Fair Presentation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Reasonably reflecting the effects of transactions</div><div class="text-supporting">FINANCIAL statements must FAIRLY present an ENTERPRISE'S financial POSITION, results OF operations, and CASH flows — APPLYING ASPE APPROPRIATELY, WITH additional DISCLOSURE where NECESSARY, is PRESUMED to RESULT in FAIR presentation IN all but EXCEPTIONAL circumstances.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Consistency of Presentation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">The same presentation and classification from period to period</div><div class="text-supporting">The PRESENTATION and CLASSIFICATION of items WITHIN the financial STATEMENTS is RETAINED from ONE period to the NEXT, UNLESS a SIGNIFICANT change in the NATURE of the enterprise'S operations, or a REVIEW of its FINANCIAL statement presentation, INDICATES that a DIFFERENT presentation or CLASSIFICATION would be MORE appropriate, OR a CHANGE is REQUIRED by another ASPE Section.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Aggregation and Materiality</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Grouping items of a similar nature, presenting material items separately</div><div class="text-supporting">EACH material CLASS of SIMILAR items is PRESENTED separately WITHIN the financial STATEMENTS — items of a DISSIMILAR nature or FUNCTION are PRESENTED separately UNLESS they are IMMATERIAL, CONSISTENT with the GENERAL materiality CONCEPT introduced in SECTION 1000 (see the COMPANION poster).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Offsetting Restrictions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Assets and liabilities, income and expenses, presented separately</div><div class="text-supporting">ASSETS and LIABILITIES, and INCOME and EXPENSES, are NOT offset AGAINST each OTHER UNLESS SPECIFICALLY permitted or REQUIRED by ANOTHER ASPE Section (SUCH as SECTION 3856'S OWN specific OFFSETTING criteria for FINANCIAL instruments).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Comparative Information</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Prior period figures presented for comparison</div><div class="text-supporting">COMPARATIVE information for the PRECEDING period is DISCLOSED for ALL amounts REPORTED in the CURRENT period'S financial STATEMENTS, UNLESS a SPECIFIC ASPE Section permits OR requires OTHERWISE — HELPING users ASSESS trends and CHANGES over TIME.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Identification of the Financial Statements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Clearly labeling what is being presented</div><div class="check-list"><span>The name of the reporting enterprise</span><span>Whether the statements cover the individual enterprise or a group of enterprises</span><span>The balance sheet date or the period covered</span><span>The presentation currency</span><span>The level of rounding used, if any</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>The Immateriality Override — a Practical Compliance Relief</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Compliance with a specific requirement is unnecessary if the effect is immaterial</div><div class="text-supporting">An ENTERPRISE need NOT comply with a PARTICULAR presentation REQUIREMENT if the EFFECT of doing SO is IMMATERIAL — a GENUINELY practical, EXPLICIT relief REFLECTING ASPE'S broader COST-benefit orientation TOWARD private-ENTERPRISE financial reporting, RATHER than REQUIRING mechanical COMPLIANCE with EVERY presentation rule REGARDLESS of ITS practical SIGNIFICANCE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Going Concern — Cross-Referenced From Section 1000</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The default basis of preparation, applied consistently</div><div class="text-supporting">FINANCIAL statements are PREPARED on a GOING concern basis UNLESS management EITHER intends to LIQUIDATE the ENTERPRISE or to CEASE trading, or HAS no REALISTIC alternative BUT to do SO — THIS Section CROSS-references the SAME going CONCERN assumption INTRODUCED in Section 1000 (see the COMPANION poster), applying IT specifically to the PRESENTATION of the FINANCIAL statements themselves.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Applying the Immateriality Override</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Skipping a technically required but practically pointless disclosure</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A genuinely trivial classification distinction</div><div class="wk-body">A small private enterprise has $800 of prepaid expenses that, strictly applying a specific presentation requirement, would need to be separately classified from a related $2,000 balance — an immaterial amount relative to the company's overall $15,000,000 balance sheet.</div><div class="wk-answer">GIVEN the AMOUNT'S genuine IMMATERIALITY relative to the COMPANY'S overall FINANCIAL statements, the COMPANY need NOT separately CLASSIFY the $800 — the IMMATERIALITY override PERMITS this PRACTICAL simplification.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — Identifying the Financial Statements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The basic labeling every statement requires</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A properly identified Balance Sheet header</div><div class="wk-body">A private manufacturing company prepares its annual Balance Sheet.</div><div class="wk-answer">The BALANCE Sheet CLEARLY states: the COMPANY'S full LEGAL name, that the STATEMENT presents the INDIVIDUAL enterprise (NOT a group), the BALANCE sheet DATE, the PRESENTATION currency (CAD), and the LEVEL of ROUNDING applied (e.g. "IN thousands of DOLLARS," if APPLICABLE) — SATISFYING the IDENTIFICATION requirements of THIS Section.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 1400 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Clear identification of the reporting enterprise, period, currency, and rounding basis</span><span>Comparative information for the preceding period</span><span>Disclosure of any change in presentation or classification, and the reason for it</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether departing from a specific presentation requirement due to immateriality is genuinely appropriate</span><span>Whether a change in presentation or classification is genuinely justified</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal — this Section is primarily a presentation, not a measurement, standard</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 1</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 1400 vs IAS 1</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 1400</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 1</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>General principles (fair presentation, consistency, offsetting)</span><span>YES</span><span>YES — broadly converged</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Statement of Comprehensive Income requirement</span><span>NOT applicable — ASPE has no OCI concept</span><span>Required — single or two-statement approach</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">BROADLY structurally SIMILAR to IAS 1, but the ABSENCE of any OCI CONCEPT under ASPE is the SINGLE most SIGNIFICANT presentation-level DIVERGENCE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where this Section's requirements apply</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Concept</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>General presentation standards</span><span>ALL primary statements</span><span>Sets the overarching framework for the entire complete set of financial statements</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1510</span><span>Current Assets and Current Liabilities — supplies the specific current/non-current classification standards this Section's general presentation framework accommodates</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1000</span><span>Financial Statement Concepts — supplies the going concern assumption and materiality concept this Section applies</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Fair presentation</div><div class="glossary-def">The requirement that financial statements reasonably reflect an enterprise's financial position, results of operations, and cash flows.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Immateriality override</div><div class="glossary-def">A practical relief permitting an enterprise to depart from a specific presentation requirement where the effect of compliance would be immaterial.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe1400 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
